The '''budget of Balboa''' is the national {{w|government budget}} of the [[Balboa|Republic of Balboa]] for the {{w|fiscal year}} 2026. Total general fund revenue is estimated at approximately $58.1 billion, against total expenditure of approximately $61.5 billion, producing an annual {{w|deficit (economics)|deficit}} of approximately $3.4 billion (0.8% of nominal {{w|Gross domestic product|GDP}}). The deficit is financed through domestic and international borrowing, within an estimated annual borrowing capacity of $6.5 billion. National debt stands at 28% of GDP ($119 billion), with annual debt service of $5.3 billion (9.1% of revenue).

Balboa operates a pseudo-welfare state model, combining high social spending — including free universal healthcare, free public education, and public housing — with a developing-country revenue base. The [[Balboa Canal|canal]] provides approximately $6.5 billion in annual toll revenue, which is fully earmarked for specific programmes including export compensation, agricultural supports, canal operations, and debt service. The canal revenue does not flow into the general fund.

Several major programmes are self-funding and operate outside the general fund: the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF), the [[Unemployment Insurance Fund]], the [[Public Housing Fund]], and the [[Hurricane Fund]].

== Revenue ==

Total general fund revenue for 2026 is estimated at approximately $58.1 billion (13.8% of nominal GDP). Revenue is derived from 25 sources, with {{w|income tax}}, {{w|Value added tax|VAT}}, {{w|land value tax}}, {{w|corporate tax}}, and {{w|customs}} duties accounting for the majority.

=== Income tax ===

{{main|Income tax in Balboa}}

Balboa levies a progressive individual income tax with a $6,000 annual exemption threshold. The tax has five brackets: 10% ($6,001–$20,000), 15% ($20,001–$35,000), 20% ($35,001–$60,000), 25% ($60,001–$100,000), and 28% (above $100,000). Approximately 54.2% of the employed workforce earns below the exemption threshold and pays no income tax. Approximately 38.9% of the workforce — approximately 6.9 million people — actually pays income tax. The top bracket ($100,000 and above, representing 0.85% of the workforce) generates approximately 42% of all income tax revenue. An estimated 15% of earners above the threshold do not pay tax due to informal-sector employment, self-employment, or undeclared income.

Estimated revenue: $16.8 billion (4.0% of GDP).

=== Value added tax ===

Balboa levies a 10% {{w|value added tax}} (VAT) at a single standard rate. Basic food items — rice, beans, cooking oil, maize, bread, milk, and eggs — and medicines are exempt. Processed and restaurant food remains subject to the standard rate. The VAT base after standard exemptions (education, healthcare, financial services, residential rent, exports) is approximately $110 billion. Gross VAT at 10% is approximately $11 billion, with collection efficiency of approximately 85%.

Estimated revenue: $8.31 billion (2.0% of GDP).

=== Land value tax ===

{{main|Land value tax in Balboa}}

A national {{w|land value tax}} (LVT) was established in 1993, replacing the previous system of property transaction taxes and stamp duties that had proven volatile during the 1978–1987 real estate bubble. The LVT taxes the unimproved value of land annually, with rates varying by zone: 2.5% for the [[Sant Cristòfor]]–[[Estret (province)|Estret]] corridor, 1.5% for other urban areas, 0.5% for agricultural (cooperative) land, and 0.3% for rural and interior areas. [[Birú]] communal lands, government property, and parks are exempt. Total estimated land value is approximately $510 billion (60% of total property value). Revenue is collected centrally and distributed to provinces and municipalities through the budget process.

Estimated revenue: $7.4 billion (1.8% of GDP).

=== Corporate tax ===

The statutory {{w|corporate tax}} rate is 25%. The effective rate is estimated at 18–20%, below the statutory rate due to reduced rates in the [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]], financial services sector preferences, cooperative and agricultural exemptions, and export and manufacturing incentives. Total corporate profits are estimated at approximately $34 billion (8.1% of nominal GDP). The financial services sector generates approximately 35% of corporate profits but pays a low effective rate of approximately 14%, consistent with the low-taxation hub strategy. Domestic manufacturing and petroleum carry a disproportionate share of the corporate tax burden.

Estimated revenue: $6.4 billion (1.5% of GDP).

=== Customs duties ===

Balboa applies the [[BTPMEU]] common external tariff on imports from outside the Balboa–Tapuya–Potocsí economic union. The tariff structure ranges from 0% on crude oil and raw fuels to 22% on fresh agricultural products and consumer goods. The [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] receives customs exemptions that reduce the effective tariff base by approximately 10%.

Estimated revenue: $6.0 billion (1.4% of GDP).

=== Excise taxes ===

{{further|Excise taxation in Balboa}}

Balboa levies {{w|excise tax|excise duties}} on fuel, alcohol, tobacco, and sugar-sweetened beverages. The fuel excise is $0.35 per litre on gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel, applied at the refinery gate or import point. The alcohol excise varies by type: $0.50 per litre on beer, $5.00 per litre on spirits, and $2.00 per litre on wine. The tobacco excise, introduced as a {{w|sin tax}} in 2014 at $5.00 per pack of 20 cigarettes, has successfully reduced the smoking rate from approximately 20% to 7% of the population, with corresponding revenue declining from a peak of $1.2 billion to approximately $154 million. The sugar-sweetened beverage tax is $0.10 per litre.

Estimated revenue: $4.4 billion (1.0% of GDP), comprising fuel ($3.5 billion), alcohol ($420 million), sugar-sweetened beverages ($280 million), and tobacco ($154 million).

=== State-owned enterprise dividends ===

The government receives {{w|dividend}}s from six state-owned enterprises (SOEs). The [[Corporació Petrolífera de l'Estat de Balboa]] (CPEB), the largest, produces approximately 185,000 barrels of oil per day and contributes approximately $1.33 billion annually. The [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) contributes $230 million, the [[Organització d'Energia Nuclear de Balboa]] (OENB) $100 million, [[Casa Balboa]] $50 million, [[Nutrició del Món]] $40 million, and [[Serveis del Canal de Balboa]] $30 million.

Estimated revenue: $1.8 billion (0.4% of GDP).

=== Property transfer tax ===

A 2% {{w|stamp duty}} is levied on real estate transactions. Total annual real estate transactions are estimated at approximately $65.6 billion.

Estimated revenue: $1.32 billion (0.3% of GDP).

=== Estate tax ===

A progressive {{w|inheritance tax|estate tax}} of 10–20% is levied on estates valued above $1 million.

Estimated revenue: $1.26 billion (0.3% of GDP).

=== FIDB distribution ===

{{main|Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa}}

The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB), the national {{w|sovereign wealth fund}} established in 1994, distributes 2% of its assets under management annually to the national budget. With AUM of approximately $65 billion, the fund achieves a blended return of approximately 7.6% and retains the balance of investment returns to compound. The 2% distribution rate is a political compromise between the [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]], which favours retaining more in the fund, and the [[Socialist Front]], which advocates higher distributions for immediate social spending.

Estimated revenue: $1.3 billion (0.3% of GDP).

=== Capital gains tax ===

A 15% {{w|capital gains tax}} is levied on realised gains. Total realised gains are estimated at approximately $5 billion annually.

Estimated revenue: $750 million (0.2% of GDP).

