== Canal revenue ==

{{main|Balboa Canal Authority}}

The [[Balboa Canal]] generates approximately $6.5 billion in annual toll revenue from approximately 14,000 vessel transits. This revenue is managed by the [[Balboa Canal Authority]] under the oversight of the [[AC Canal Commission]] and is fully earmarked. It does not flow into the general fund.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Allocation !! Amount !! Share
|-
| [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|Negative tariff]] (export compensation) || $1.1 billion || 17%
|-
| Agricultural price supports and cooperative assistance || $1.2 billion || 18%
|-
| Canal operations and maintenance || $1.5 billion || 23%
|-
| FIDB contribution || $0.3 billion || 5%
|-
| Other earmarks || $0.4 billion || 6%
|-
| Debt service (redirected) || $2.0 billion || 31%
|-
| '''Total''' || '''$6.5 billion''' || '''100%'''
|}

The redirection of $2.0 billion in canal revenue to debt service reduces the government's borrowing requirements. The FIDB contribution was reduced from approximately $0.8 billion to $0.3 billion, as the fund's AUM of $65 billion can grow from investment returns without large contributions.

== Expenditure ==

Total general fund expenditure for 2026 is estimated at approximately $61.5 billion (14.6% of nominal GDP). The largest expenditure categories are education and healthcare, which together account for approximately 46% of spending.

=== Education ===

{{main|Education in Balboa}}

Education is the largest single expenditure at approximately $14.8 billion (3.5% of GDP). The system employs 552,000 teachers and faculty across primary, secondary, and post-secondary levels. Teacher base salaries are set at $10,000 and adjusted by the [[provincial salary multiplier]] framework. Eleven public universities hold combined endowments of $7.0 billion, generating approximately $352 million in annual spending (at a 5% payout rule) that offsets the budget.

=== Healthcare ===

{{main|National Health Service (Balboa)}}

The [[National Health Service (Balboa)|National Health Service]] (NHS) is the second-largest expenditure at approximately $13.6 billion (3.2% of GDP). Balboa operates a {{w|Beveridge Model|Beveridge-model}} healthcare system: the government owns hospitals, employs healthcare workers, and provides free care at the point of use. Universal coverage extends to all 35 million residents. The NHS employs 238,000 healthcare workers in a three-tier salary system: provincial-scale salaries for general practitioners, regular specialists, nurses, and technicians; and global-rate salaries for super-specialist doctors ($100,000–$350,000) and super-specialist nurses ($60,000–$100,000) who are not subject to the provincial multiplier. Personnel costs account for 30% of the NHS budget, with the remainder allocated to pharmaceuticals, hospital operations, equipment, and public health programmes.

=== Debt service ===

National debt stands at 28% of GDP ($119 billion). Annual debt service is $5.3 billion (9.1% of revenue), comprising $2.0 billion from canal revenue redirected to debt service and $3.3 billion from borrowing. The blended interest rate is 4.5%, reflecting a mix of domestic bonds (4.5–5%), international sovereign bonds (6%), AC concessional loans (2%), and ACMF emergency lending (3%). The debt service ratio of 9.1% of revenue is within the 10% threshold generally considered comfortable for sovereign borrowers.

=== National police ===

{{main|National Police of Balboa}}

The National Police Force operates with a budget of approximately $5.1 billion (1.2% of GDP) and employs 70,000 officers, a ratio of one officer per 500 residents. The force is national in structure and separate from the [[Balboa Defence Force]]. Provincial salary multipliers apply to all personnel. Internal affairs and oversight, mandated by the [[Twelve Guarantees]], account for approximately 4% of the police budget. Special units address drug trafficking, financial crime, and border security. Personnel costs account for 22% of the budget, with the remainder allocated to vehicles, equipment, facilities, technology, and training.

=== Civil service ===

The ministerial government employs 95,000 civil servants across 27 ministries and agencies, with a total budget of approximately $5.1 billion (1.2% of GDP). Provincial salary multipliers apply. Personnel costs account for 45% of the civil service budget, with the remainder allocated to facilities, IT systems, official travel, professional services, and government procurement administration.

=== Canal earmarks ===

Canal-related expenditure funded from earmarked canal revenue (excluding the $2.0 billion debt service redirect) totals approximately $4.5 billion. This includes the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] export compensation regime ($1.1 billion), agricultural price supports and cooperative assistance ($1.2 billion), canal operations and maintenance ($1.5 billion), FIDB contribution ($0.3 billion), and other earmarks ($0.4 billion).

=== Infrastructure ===

Infrastructure expenditure is approximately $3.0 billion (0.7% of GDP), covering national highway maintenance, rural road programmes, airport subsidies, water and sewage systems, and port infrastructure. Major new projects — including nuclear power, large ports, and high-speed rail — are funded separately through infrastructure bonds, AC concessional loans, and SOE capital expenditure.

=== Defence ===

{{main|Balboa Defence Force}}

The [[Balboa Defence Force]] (BDF) operates with a budget of approximately $2.4 billion (0.6% of GDP), with approximately 30,000 active personnel and 3,375 civilian staff.

=== Provincial transfers ===

Central government transfers to the five provinces and their municipalities total approximately $2.0 billion (0.5% of GDP). Education, healthcare, police, and civil service are funded nationally; transfers are for municipal operations and local infrastructure only. The transfer formula has three components: a base per capita allocation (40%), an equalisation component weighted toward poorer provinces (35%), and a municipal operating component weighted by urbanisation (25%).

