=== Social welfare ===

Cash social welfare programmes total approximately $1.6 billion (0.4% of GDP). Most welfare is delivered in kind — free healthcare, free education, school meals, and public housing — with cash programmes serving as a supplementary safety net. Programmes include disability benefits ($491 million, 409,500 recipients), child and family allowances ($555 million, means-tested for the bottom 30%), direct cash transfers ($495 million, bottom 12%), and emergency relief ($80 million).

=== Contingency fund ===

A contingency reserve of approximately $1.5 billion (0.4% of GDP) is maintained for natural disaster response, economic shocks, public health emergencies, and unforeseen infrastructure failures.

=== Ministry discretionary programmes ===

Twenty individual ministry programme line items total approximately $1.4 billion (0.3% of GDP), funding agricultural extension, cultural programmes, environmental protection, sports, industrial development, tourism promotion, diplomatic missions, science and technology, indigenous affairs, and other sector-specific initiatives.

=== SOE subsidies ===

Government subsidies to state-owned enterprises for non-commercial public service obligations total approximately $285 million. The largest subsidy is to the [[Serveis de Comunicacions de Balboa]] (SCB) for public broadcasting ($80 million), followed by the [[Organització d'Energia Nuclear de Balboa]] (OENB) for rural electrification ($60 million), the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) for affordable housing ($50 million), the [[Corporació Petrolífera de l'Estat de Balboa]] (CPEB) for strategic petroleum reserves ($40 million), [[Casa Balboa]] for rural retail operations ($30 million), and [[Nutrició del Món]] (NDM) for mineral exploration and mine safety ($25 million). The net fiscal impact of SOEs is positive: dividends of $1.8 billion minus subsidies of $285 million yields a net contribution of $1.5 billion to the general fund.

=== AC membership dues ===

Annual membership dues to the [[Adonian Community]] total $75 million, with an additional $25 million in contributions to international organisations, for a total of $100 million.

=== Other expenditure ===

Additional expenditure includes research and development and AC contributions ($500 million) and the government contribution to the [[Hurricane Fund]] ($150 million).

== Provincial salary multiplier framework ==

A provincial salary multiplier system applies to all public sector employees — education, healthcare, police, and civil service. Base salaries are set nationally and adjusted by a multiplier reflecting the cost of living and labour market conditions in each province:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Province !! Multiplier !! Description
|-
| [[Estret (province)|Estret]] || 1.40x || [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Portnou]] — canal corridor, financial hub
|-
| [[Jala (province)|Jala]] || 1.10x || [[Sant Bart]], [[Badia Curta]] — industrial
|-
| [[Migjorn (province)|Migjorn]] || 0.90x || Agricultural interior
|-
| [[Nurra (province)|Nurra]] || 0.85x || [[Novara]], rural
|-
| [[Tramuntana (province)|Tramuntana]] || 0.80x || Interior highlands
|}

Super-specialist doctors and nurses in the NHS are paid at global rates ($100,000–$350,000 for doctors, $60,000–$100,000 for nurses) and are not subject to the provincial multiplier. These super-specialists are concentrated at the Sant Cristòfor teaching hospital and the Sant Bart interior university hospital.

== Self-funding programmes ==

Several major programmes operate outside the general fund through dedicated revenue sources:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Programme !! Funding source !! Annual revenue !! Annual expenditure
|-
| [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF) || 12% payroll tax (6% employee + 6% employer) || $13.1 billion || $11.3 billion (pensions)
|-
| [[Unemployment Insurance Fund]] || 1% payroll tax (0.5% employee + 0.5% employer) || $1.36 billion || $420 million (benefits)
|-
| [[Public Housing Fund]] || Tiered construction fees on new development ($5–$30/sqm by province) || $489 million || ~30,000 units/year
|-
| [[Hurricane Fund]] || Resort tax ($262M) + government contribution ($150M) || $412 million || Disaster response reserve
|}

The BPF is the second-largest institutional investor in Balboa after the FIDB, with AUM of approximately $25 billion. The annual surplus of approximately $3.6 billion is retained by the fund to build reserves for future demographic pressure. The Unemployment Insurance Fund retains its surplus of approximately $940 million as a reserve. The Public Housing Fund constructs approximately 30,000 units per year at an average cost of $16,300 per unit, targeting a public housing stock of 15–20% of households. The Hurricane Fund targets a reserve of approximately $4.1 billion over ten years, sufficient for one major hurricane at $2–5 billion in damage.

